# Flutter/Dart security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to Flutter/Dart-
specific risks and the safe APIs to use instead. Applies only to `*.dart`
files.

## Secret and credential storage

- Store a token, session cookie, refresh token, or any other credential in
  `flutter_secure_storage` (backed by Keychain on iOS, Keystore/
  EncryptedSharedPreferences on Android), never in `shared_preferences` —
  `SharedPreferences` persists as an unencrypted plist/XML file on disk
  that any process with filesystem access (a rooted device, a backup
  extraction) can read directly.
- `shared_preferences` remains fine for genuinely non-sensitive
  preferences (a theme choice, a "seen onboarding" flag) — the rule is
  about what the value *is*, not a blanket ban on the package.
- Never hard-code an API key, client secret, or signing credential as a
  Dart string literal compiled into the app — every string in a release
  build is recoverable from the compiled binary with straightforward
  reverse-engineering tools. Load it from a backend-issued short-lived
  token, a build-time `--dart-define`/`.env` mechanism the project already
  uses that keeps the actual secret server-side, or a secrets manager
  reached over an authenticated network call — not compiled directly into
  client code.

## Network calls and certificate pinning

- Use `https://` for every network call that carries user data,
  credentials, or tokens; a plaintext `http://` endpoint is trivially
  interceptable on a shared or compromised network.
- For a call carrying especially sensitive data (payment, health,
  authentication) where the project has decided the risk of a
  compromised/misissued CA in the platform trust store is worth guarding
  against, use certificate pinning (`HttpClient.badCertificateCallback`
  validated against a known pin, or a package such as
  `http_certificate_pinning`/platform-native pinning) — but treat this as
  a deliberate, project-level decision with a rotation plan, not a default
  to apply to every request, since a mishandled pin update can lock out
  legitimate traffic when a certificate rotates.
- Never override `badCertificateCallback` (or an equivalent HTTP client
  hook) to unconditionally return `true` — that disables certificate
  validation entirely for that client, the same failure class as Go's
  `InsecureSkipVerify: true`, and is never appropriate outside a
  short-lived local test.

## Platform channel and input validation

- Validate and sanitize any value crossing a `MethodChannel`/
  `EventChannel` in either direction — a payload from native code is
  external input from the Dart side's perspective, and a payload from
  Dart is external input from the native side's perspective; do not trust
  a channel argument's shape or range without checking it, especially
  before using it to index a collection, build a file path, or construct
  a query.
- Encode structured data crossing a platform channel with the codec the
  channel already uses (the default standard codec, or an explicit
  `MessageCodec`) rather than hand-rolling string concatenation/parsing
  across the channel boundary, which reintroduces the same injection and
  parsing-ambiguity risks as building a query string by hand.

## Local storage and logging

- Never write a secret, token, or PII value to `debugPrint`/`print` or any
  logging sink that a release build still ships with enabled — a log
  statement written for local debugging has a way of surviving into a
  shipped build and into crash-reporting payloads.
- A `WebView` rendering any content that includes user-controlled or
  externally-fetched HTML must not run with JavaScript enabled on
  untrusted content unless the content is sanitized first — the same XSS
  risk class as an unescaped web template, just inside a native app.

## Dependency hygiene

- Run `flutter pub outdated` and review a dependency's changelog/CVE
  history before a major-version bump, and prefer `dart pub deps` to
  understand what a new transitive dependency actually pulls in before
  adding it.
